The "oh." mark and Inter
------------------------
The letterforms in the ohmail icon were drawn as outlined paths from Inter Bold
(Inter 4.1, copyright (c) 2016 The Inter Project Authors,
https://github.com/rsms/inter), licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1.

No font binary is distributed in this repository. The .ttf used to draw the
mark stayed with the design files, alongside its OFL text, and is not part of
this tree. The guard check (.github/scripts/assert-pr-guard.sh) refuses any pull
request whose tree carries a file of a font kind, so one cannot ride along by
accident.

That distinction is the whole answer. The OFL's conditions — its copyleft, its
requirement that the licence travel with the font, and its Reserved Font Name
rules — attach to redistribution of the Font Software. This repository
redistributes none, so none of them are triggered here. The icon files are
artwork whose curves were derived from Inter's outlines; our reading,
consistent with how the OFL and SIL's own FAQ treat work produced *with* a font
rather than copies *of* the font software, is that such artwork is not itself
Font Software and is not encumbered by the OFL. That is our position, stated
plainly, not a court's ruling.

If a font binary is ever shipped from this repository, this section becomes
wrong the moment it happens, and stays wrong until the OFL text ships beside it
and the Reserved Font Name "Inter" is honoured. Credit is given here either
way, because Inter is excellent and the mark would not look the way it does
without it.


Per-file licence headers: deliberately absent
---------------------------------------------
There is no licence header at the top of each source file. That is a decision,
not an oversight, and there are two reasons.

The ordinary one: a single LICENSE plus this file is the normal and sufficient
arrangement for an application repository. The FSF recommends per-file notices
so a file separated from its project still carries its terms — a real risk for
a library whose files get vendored into other projects, a small one for an
application nobody copies a single file out of.

The one specific to ohmail: several of the trees published here —
packages/tokens, packages/ui, packages/fixtures, packages/client-engine and
apps/webapp/app — are the *same files* the hosted ohmail Cloud web client is
built from. TrafficFlow GmbH holds the copyright on all of it and licenses it
both ways: AGPL-3.0 in this repository, under its own terms in the Cloud
product. Stamping an AGPL header into each of those files would assert, inside
the Cloud client's own sources, a licence that does not describe them.
Declaring the licence once, at the root, for the repository as published,
states exactly the thing that is true: *this tree, as published here, is
AGPL-3.0.*

If you extract a file from this repository, it came from here and it is
AGPL-3.0. Please carry that with you.


Why AGPL-3.0
------------
This client is free and is meant to stay free. AGPL-3.0 keeps it that way:
anyone may use, study, change and redistribute it, and any redistributed
change comes back under the same licence — so a closed-source re-skin of
ohmail is not possible. The Affero clause closes the hosting loophole as
well: anyone who modifies this code and runs it as a network service for
other people must offer those users the modified source, so a closed-source
hosted copy is not possible either.

TrafficFlow GmbH holds the copyright on this codebase and can therefore also
license it differently for its own products — the hosted ohmail Cloud tier.
Contributions are accepted under AGPL-3.0 with a DCO sign-off, with no
copyright assignment and no CLA. CONTRIBUTING.md explains what that means in
practice, including the one part of the tree where it genuinely constrains
what we can accept.


